// Policy module for a codemode agent: files under the roots the tool named,
// nothing else. The agent's program cannot change this decision and cannot reach
// it: the guard (`host/guard.eyg`) holds the resolved policy and applies it to
// every effect the program performs.
//
// This module exports one function, `rules`, and performs nothing: the tool hands
// the roots to it as a value, because a module imported by the guarded entry
// cannot ask the host for anything (an effect at a module's top level is refused
// before the guard is installed). `ctx` is the record the tool writes into the
// entry it generates for a call:
//
//   roots   absolute paths the agent may work in, as a list of strings. An empty
//           list means no roots, so every file effect is refused - name the roots
//           you want, or use `deny-all.eyg` when you mean none. The pi tool
//           (`pi-eyg-codemode`) supplies the session working directory here when
//           nothing is configured, which is why a first pi session can read and
//           write its own workspace and nothing else.
//   cwd     the working directory the program runs in. The guard has already
//           turned a relative path into an absolute one before a rule sees it, so
//           a rule that does not care where the program was can ignore this.
//   fields  not used by this policy; `spawner-effects.eyg` reads it.
//
// A name in `secret_directory_names`, and the conventional secret file name
// `.env.eyg`, are unreachable in every direction: not readable, not listable, not
// searchable, not writable, not deletable, even under a root. That is a narrowing of
// the roots rule, not a change of it - the roots decision below is the same one it
// always was, and these paths are refused on top of it. The reason it is a directory
// test and not only a name test is measured on `notes/pi-codemode-threat-model.md`:
// a suffix test on one extension is beaten by the spelling of a name (`.ENV.EYG`,
// `.env.eyg.`, `.env.eyg ` all landed against the rule that only asked whether the
// path ended in `.env.eyg`), while a directory name a path has to pass through is
// not something a file's own name can dodge.
//
// Network access is refused here: `fetch` and the header rules are the fields
// another policy of yours would set to open a host or inject a token.
//
// This module is self-contained: the policy is written out here rather than
// imported, so the package carries no code from outside itself and the whole
// decision is readable where it is used. `notes/pi-codemode-policy-inlining.md`
// records where the policy came from and how it was checked.
//
// The decision, one rule for each of the 19 effects a policy can decide (the 23 effects
// in `guides/eyg-surface.tsv` less the 4 no rule can forbid). Paths arrive
// absolute, with no `.` or `..` segment: the guard resolves them first.
//
//   UNDER THE ROOTS (path checked against `roots`), and never a hidden name
//     ReadFile, ReadDirectory, WriteFile, AppendFile, MakeDirectory, DeleteFile,
//     Search                              - a search is one decision for the root
//                                            it starts in, and the guard lists
//                                            each directory it walks through the
//                                            `read_directory` rule as it goes
//   HIDDEN NAME, refused under a root or not
//     every path effect, for a path with a segment in `secret_directory_names` or
//     ending `.env.eyg`                   - see `hidden` below
//   PERFORMED
//     CWD                                 - where the program is, it changes nothing
//     Now, Random, Sleep, Flip            - values the program already could have
//     StandardOut, StandardError          - output of the program itself
//     Exit                                - ends the program; Pass may rewrite the
//                                            status, Mock cannot (no return value)
//   REFUSED
//     Fetch                               - the network
//     StandardIn                          - the input meant for the chat
//     CreateKey                           - a fresh opaque key
//   ANSWERED AS UNSET, not refused
//     Env                                 - Mock(None({})): Env returns Option, so the
//                                            program cannot read the caller's
//                                            environment (which holds the tokens)
//
// Anything outside `roots` gets `Error("<effect> denied by policy: <path>")`, and
// a program that ignores that value carries on with what it got - a denial is a
// value, not a crash. A hidden name gets the same text with why: the program can
// tell a refused path from a refused name.

// Perform the effect with the value the program asked for, unchanged.
let pass = (value) -> {
  Pass(value)
}

// Refuse, with the text the program receives.
let deny = (reason) -> {
  (_) -> { Mock(reason) }
}

let append = (a, b) -> {
  !string_append(a, b)
}

let refuse = (effect) -> {
  deny(append(effect, " denied by policy"))
}

// Is the absolute path equal to or inside the absolute root.
let is_under = (root, path) -> {
  let root = match !string_ends_with(root, "/") {
    True(_) -> { root }
    False(_) -> { append(root, "/") }
  }
  match !equal(append(path, "/"), root) {
    True(_) -> { True({}) }
    False(_) -> { !string_starts_with(path, root) }
  }
}

let any_under = (roots, path) -> {
  !list_fold(roots, False({}), (root, found) -> {
    match found {
      True(_) -> { True({}) }
      False(_) -> { is_under(root, path) }
    }
  })
}

// The refusal names the effect and the path, which is what makes a denial
// readable to the program that asked.
let denied = (effect, path) -> {
  append(effect, append(" denied by policy: ", path))
}

// A hidden name gets the why as well as the where, so a program can tell that its
// path was under a root and the name is what the call refused.
let hidden_denied = (effect, path) -> {
  append(denied(effect, path), " (a name this policy hides)")
}

// Allow a file effect only for paths under one of the absolute roots.
// `path_of` takes the path out of the effect's value, which differs by effect.
let under = (effect, roots, path_of) -> {
  (value) -> {
    let path = path_of(value)
    match any_under(roots, path) {
      True(_) -> { Pass(value) }
      False(_) -> { Mock(denied(effect, path)) }
    }
  }
}

// The value of DeleteFile, MakeDirectory and ReadDirectory is the path itself.
let path_of_value = (path) -> {
  path
}

// ReadFile, WriteFile, AppendFile and Search are given a record with a `path` row.
let request_path = (request) -> {
  request.path
}

// The directory names a call never reaches. They are data, not code: an operator who
// wants one of them open, or another one closed, copies this policy and edits this
// list and nothing else.
let secret_directory_names = [".ssh", ".gnupg", ".aws", "credentials", "secrets"]

// The file name that carries credentials by convention in this ecosystem.
let secret_file_suffix = ".env.eyg"

// Is this path segment one of the hidden directory names.
let hidden_name = (segment) -> {
  !list_fold(secret_directory_names, False({}), (name, found) -> {
    match found {
      True(_) -> { True({}) }
      False(_) -> { !equal(name, segment) }
    }
  })
}

// Does the absolute path pass through a hidden directory, or end in the secret file
// name. The segments come from splitting on `/`: a path arrives absolute, so the
// first segment is empty and the last is the file's own name. The test is on
// segments, so a hidden name hides that directory and everything under it - and a
// *file* named `secrets` is hidden too, which is the cost of a rule that never looks
// inside a path.
let hidden = (path) -> {
  let parts = !string_split(path, "/")
  let in_hidden_directory = !list_fold(parts.tail, hidden_name(parts.head), (segment, found) -> {
    match found {
      True(_) -> { True({}) }
      False(_) -> { hidden_name(segment) }
    }
  })
  match in_hidden_directory {
    True(_) -> { True({}) }
    False(_) -> { !string_ends_with(path, secret_file_suffix) }
  }
}

// Wrap a rule so a hidden name is refused before the roots are consulted: the name
// is refused under a root and outside one alike, which is what makes the wrapped
// rule narrower than the rule it wraps rather than a different decision.
let hide_secrets = (effect, path_of, rule) -> {
  (value) -> {
    let path = path_of(value)
    match hidden(path) {
      True(_) -> { Mock(hidden_denied(effect, path)) }
      False(_) -> { rule(value) }
    }
  }
}

let rules = (ctx) -> {
  let roots = ctx.roots
  {
    append_file: hide_secrets("append_file", request_path, under("append_file", roots, request_path)),
    create_key: refuse("create_key"),
    cwd: pass,
    delete_file: hide_secrets("delete_file", path_of_value, under("delete_file", roots, path_of_value)),
    env: (_) -> { Mock(None({})) },
    exit: pass,
    fetch: refuse("fetch"),
    flip: pass,
    make_directory: hide_secrets("make_directory", path_of_value, under("make_directory", roots, path_of_value)),
    now: pass,
    random: pass,
    read_directory: hide_secrets("read_directory", path_of_value, under("read_directory", roots, path_of_value)),
    read_file: hide_secrets("read_file", request_path, under("read_file", roots, request_path)),
    search: hide_secrets("search", request_path, under("search", roots, request_path)),
    sleep: pass,
    standard_error: pass,
    standard_in: refuse("standard_in"),
    standard_out: pass,
    write_file: hide_secrets("write_file", request_path, under("write_file", roots, request_path))
  }
}

{rules}
